{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01F6o8y2KFrdHqoMRTGyqGpC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted results for every \"planned\" experiment in the experiment_plan, under the \"best\" tier scenario. I need to provide result_rationale for each prediction explaining why this result would occur under the best scenario.\n\nLet me identify all planned experiments:\n\n1. **exp-ctrl-contam-pilot** (status: planned) - but wait, it also appears in registry as DONE. Let me re-check... The experiment_plan says \"planned\" but registry says DONE. I should follow the experiment_plan status field.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the experiment_plan statuses:\n- exp-two-sided: DONE\n\n\n- exp-extended-sim: DONE\n- exp-ctrl-contam-pilot: planned (but registry shows DONE)\n- exp-ctrl-contam: planned\n- exp-empirical-null: DONE\n- exp-power-theory: DONE\n- exp-leaderboard-data-recon: DONE\n- exp-leaderboard-audit: planned (ABANDONED)\n- exp-natural-experiment: planned (but registry shows DONE)\n- exp-v1-irt-refit-expanded: planned (but registry shows DONE)\n- exp-4pl-ablation: planned (but registry shows DONE)\n- exp-baseline-comparison: planned\n\nSo the truly planned experiments I need to generate predictions for are: exp-ctrl-contam-pilot, exp-ctrl-contam, exp-leaderboard-audit, exp-natural-experiment, exp-v1-irt-refit-expanded, exp-4pl-ablation, and exp-baseline-comparison. There's some discrepancy between the experiment_plan and registry statuses, but I'll prioritize the experiment_plan as the source of truth. Looking at the planned experiments in the plan, I see there are quite a few—some already showing results in the registry while others remain pending. The user is asking me to expand detailed data for each planned experiment, which I interpret as generating predictions for all of them under the best-case scenario, where Post-RL successfully differentiates the lz* models from baselines and all our core claims hold at full strength.\n\nThe best tier scenario hinges on several key outcomes: lz* models maintaining strong detection signals after GRPO training while HT and other baselines degrade significantly, ConStat and MIA baselines failing to match lz* performance, ARC-C showing detectable effects even with fixed parameters, and Mistral results replicating across multiple seeds to validate the third model family. I need to map these assumptions onto specific metric ranges—post-RL lz* Cohen's d between 2.0 and 4.0, post-RL HT between 0.0 and 1.0, and similar bounds for the other key measurements.\n\nNow I'm working through the planned experiments to generate best-tier predictions for each one, keeping in mind that some experiments already have completed status while others are still planned. Looking at the pilot experiment, I've got Qwen 3.5-9B across four conditions with MMLU data that's partially in the registry—under best-case assumptions, controlled contamination should show strong detection signals while clean comparisons stay near zero. The full controlled experiment scales this up to four model families across six contamination levels and three benchmarks, where I'd expect consistent strong detection across all families with Mistral replicating across multiple seeds. The leaderboard audit is abandoned so I'll note that but include it anyway. The natural experiment with base-to-chat model pairs is already done in the registry. The IRT refit experiment is also concluded in the registry with negative results, so that's settled. The 4PL ablation is similarly already in the registry.\n\nThe baseline comparison experiment is done with success, now expanded under best tier to include ConStat, MIA, and perplexity baselines. I need to think about the cross-benchmark aggregated lz* statistic and how that fits. The post-RL experiment with GRPO after SFT contamination is the critical one for differentiating best-tier results. Then there's the extra benchmarks covering BBH and MATH, plus the domain analysis which is already done successfully in the registry.\n\nNow I'm focusing on what \"best\" actually means for each—it's really about post-RL differentiation. I should generate the YAML output with specific metrics and clear rationales for each experiment.\n\nThe core narrative for best tier is that IRT person-fit is structurally robust to post-RL shifts, being the only binary-only method that survives when log-prob methods and simpler statistics like HT fail under distributional change. The 4PL parameterization gives both detection power and RL robustness that difficulty-only approaches can't match.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the specific predictions for each experiment under this best-tier scenario. The remaining experiments cover cross-benchmark aggregation, post-RL robustness, additional benchmarks to fill power gaps, and domain-level contamination profiles. Now I'm grounding my predictions in the actual registry data—looking at what we've already measured for Qwen's clean and contaminated lz* scores to calibrate my estimates for the other experiments. Looking at the performance metrics across these models and benchmarks, I'm seeing the MIA AUROC complete at 0.59 for SFT and the post-RL analysis showing failed status. Now I'm mapping out what the best-case scenario would look like across the three experiments—validating three to four model families with Mistral replicated across additional seeds, expecting large effect sizes on ARC-C and post-GRPO metrics, and projecting baseline comparisons where we beat five to seven existing approaches with ConStat AUROC in the 0.55-0.7 range.\n\nLet me structure the YAML predictions more carefully. For the pilot contamination experiment, I'm expecting the go/no-go to succeed decisively with clean-versus-clean differences at noise floor, strong Cohen's d above 3.0 at 15% contamination, and memorization fractions around 35-38%. For the full contamination experiment across all model and benchmark combinations, I'm projecting all four families to show large effect sizes above 2.0 on MMLU at 15% contamination, with Mistral showing consistent negative shifts around -6.5 across seeds and a strong d value near -4.\n\nThe leaderboard audit is abandoned, but the natural experiment should show systematic SFT effects on HellaSwag and GSM8K under the best tier. The IRT refit experiment remains negative since it's about classical estimation rather than detection. For the 4PL ablation, I'm expecting the four-parameter model to deliver 14-27 percentage point power improvements with well-calibrated false alarm rates, making it essential to include.\n\nNow looking at the baseline comparison—my approach beats 5-7 existing methods, and at the model level I'm seeing effect sizes above 2.9. The hypothesis test performs comparably to my method before reinforcement learning, though post-RL data would help differentiate them. Accuracy z-scores are sensitive but lack specificity, while ConStat needs retesting with rephrased items to improve its 0.55-0.7 AUROC range. The MIA and Min-K% methods show marginal performance on SFT data and near-random results post-RL, and PPL-based approaches fail entirely after reinforcement learning. Guttman and U3 indices confirm smaller effect sizes around 0.43. Cross-benchmark aggregation in the multiscale approach improves detection overall, which strengthens the case for this framework.\n\nThe key differentiator is what happens post-RL: my method retains most of its signal with effect sizes between 2.5-3.5, while the hypothesis test degrades significantly to 0.3-0.8, and MIA collapses to near-random performance around 0.48-0.53 AUROC. Accuracy z-scores still detect something trivially, but this post-RL behavior is what really sets my approach apart. I'm also filling detection gaps with larger benchmarks—BBH provides 6500+ items covering the 1K-10K range with 70-85% power at 15% contamination, and MATH adds another 5000 items with around 60% power.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the YAML with precise numbers and clear rationales connecting these results back to the core assumptions.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":26683,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":26683},"output_tokens":29,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"950a7b8d-5c20-43a0-a03c-d6a54a9f5f4c","uuid":"846059ff-cf07-449f-8dda-be36083b1b34"}
